%S A396406 1,3,5,8,13,21,34,55,89,144,225,351,554,875,1345,2066,3203,4971,7574,
%T A396406 11543,17683,27108,41067,62263,94622,143881,217101,327832,495443,
%U A396406 749195,1127236,1697179,2554961,3848384,5777651,8679441,13031206,19574659,29338781
%N A396406 Number of distinct affine isometries at word-length n in the side-reflection group of a right triangle in the plane whose legs have transcendental ratio; independent of the choice of triangle.
%C A396406 Given a right triangle whose two legs have transcendental ratio, reflecting it across each of its three sides generates a group W. Define a(n) as the number of elements in W that require exactly n of those reflections to express, meaning they cannot be written with fewer. The transcendental condition makes the count the same for every such triangle, at every depth.
%C A396406 If the legs are rational instead, the counts agree with those here up to a depth that depends on the shape, and are strictly smaller beyond it. The terms shown were computed on the (3,4,5) triangle, which agrees with the universal sequence through at least n = 42; the (1,2) triangle first deviates earlier, at n = 33, where it gives 3848354 instead of a(33) = 3848384. The link gives a bound on where the first deviation occurs.
%C A396406 For n = 1 through 9, the values are 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, 34, 55, 89, 144, which match A000045(n+3). At n = 10 the agreement breaks: a(10) = 225, while A000045(13) = 233.
%C A396406 As for growth, the ratio a(n+1)/a(n) converges to about 1.4916177871. That is less than the golden ratio, so the early Fibonacci resemblance does not reflect the actual asymptotic growth rate.
%H A396406 Vico Bonfioli, <a href="https://github.com/ElVec1o/pythagorean-reflection-sequence">The Universal Right-Triangle Reflection Sequence (Unequal Legs)</a>, 2026.
%H A396406 Vico Bonfioli, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20370090">Code, paper, and verification data</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A396406 Vico Bonfioli, <a href="https://github.com/ElVec1o/pythagorean-reflection-sequence/tree/main/code/rust_bfs">Rust program</a> (disk-streaming BFS implementation used to compute a(0) through a(38)).
%H A396406 Vico Bonfioli, <a href="https://github.com/ElVec1o/pythagorean-reflection-sequence/blob/main/paper/journal/paper1.pdf">The universal right-triangle reflection sequence: a word-length metric, effective universality, and the lamplighter structure of A396406</a>, 2026.
%H A396406 S. Isola and R. Piergallini, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1405.1881">On the generic triangle group</a>, arXiv:1405.1881 [math.MG], 2014-2015.
%F A396406 a(n) = A000045(n+3) for 1 <= n <= 9.
%F A396406 a(n) <= [t^n] (1+t)^2/(1-t-t^2) for all n >= 0 (Steinberg upper bound), with strict inequality for n >= 10.
%F A396406 The exponential growth rate is exactly beta_2 = 1.4916177871143742268..., the reciprocal square root of the least positive root of Sigma_1(q) = 1 for the travel-block generating function Sigma_1; a(n)^(1/n) -> beta_2, and in particular the rate is not 3/2 (Bonfioli 2026).
%o A396406 (Python)
%o A396406 # BFS (Breadth-First Search) on the side-reflection group of a right triangle, in exact rational
%o A396406 # arithmetic. Any right triangle with unequal legs matches the universal
%o A396406 # sequence through depth 32, so for d_max <= 32 the choice of legs is
%o A396406 # immaterial; we use (a, b) = (1, 2).
%o A396406 from fractions import Fraction
%o A396406 def A_2D_universal(d_max=20):
%o A396406     O, I = Fraction(0), Fraction(1)
%o A396406     V = [(O, O), (I, O), (O, I + I)]
%o A396406     sides = [(0, 1), (1, 2), (2, 0)]
%o A396406     def reflection(p0, p1):
%o A396406         dx, dy = p1[0] - p0[0], p1[1] - p0[1]
%o A396406         L = dx*dx + dy*dy
%o A396406         a = (dx*dx - dy*dy) / L
%o A396406         b = 2*dx*dy / L
%o A396406         d = -a
%o A396406         tx = (1 - a)*p0[0] - b*p0[1]
%o A396406         ty = -b*p0[0] + (1 - d)*p0[1]
%o A396406         return (a, b, b, d, tx, ty)
%o A396406     def compose(M, N):
%o A396406         a1, b1, c1, d1, tx1, ty1 = M
%o A396406         a2, b2, c2, d2, tx2, ty2 = N
%o A396406         return (a1*a2 + b1*c2, a1*b2 + b1*d2,
%o A396406                 c1*a2 + d1*c2, c1*b2 + d1*d2,
%o A396406                 a1*tx2 + b1*ty2 + tx1,
%o A396406                 c1*tx2 + d1*ty2 + ty1)
%o A396406     gens = [reflection(V[i], V[j]) for i, j in sides]
%o A396406     identity = (I, O, O, I, O, O)
%o A396406     seen = {identity}; frontier = {identity}; seq = [1]
%o A396406     for _ in range(d_max):
%o A396406         new = set()
%o A396406         for M in frontier:
%o A396406             for g in gens:
%o A396406                 N = compose(g, M)
%o A396406                 if N not in seen:
%o A396406                     new.add(N); seen.add(N)
%o A396406         seq.append(len(new)); frontier = new
%o A396406     return seq
%o A396406 (Rust) // See Links
%Y A396406 Cf. A000045 (Fibonacci; equals a(n) for 1 <= n <= 9).
%Y A396406 Cf. A008576 (coordination sequence for planar net 4.8.8; coincides with the orbit-growth sequence of the isosceles right triangle, which is strictly smaller than a(n) for n >= 4 due to an extra leg-swap stabilizer).
%Y A396406 Cf. A397439, A397437, A396927, A397438, A396953 (the 3D-7D orthoscheme growth series, a related reflection-group-growth family by the same author).
